<Project>

  <!-- Everything the three projects here agree about, stated once.

       MSBuild walks *up* from each project looking for this file, so a checkout of
       Fjord sitting inside somebody else's workspace would otherwise be told what to do
       by a file that has never heard of it. That is the same hazard `Boxops.Fjord.Indexer`
       already guards with `ManagePackageVersionsCentrally=false`, and the answer is the
       same: say it here, close to the projects it is about. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- No framework here on purpose. The library multi-targets for reach; the two
         console programs pin one, because `dotnet run` against a project with several
         cannot tell which was meant. -->
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>

    <!-- Nothing is packable unless it says so, and two things here say so: the client
         library, and the indexer as a **tool** package. The demo producer and the SCIP
         reader stay unpackable — they are arguments about the protocol rather than
         things to install.

         The distinction that matters is library versus tool, not library versus
         everything: a consumer referencing `Boxops.Fjord.Client` is writing a producer,
         and a consumer installing `fjord-indexer` is running one. Both are ways of
         getting something this repository builds without cloning it. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!-- **A default, not a lockstep.** This is the version a package gets when its own
         project does not state one, and it tracks the Rust workspace's so that the
         ordinary case needs no decision. A project may override it, and one does.

         Lockstep was the arrangement until it was measured: of the six artifacts this
         repository publishes, exactly **one** embeds a schema fingerprint. `fjord-client`
         computes it from the schema its caller supplies and `Boxops.Fjord.Client` takes it
         from the schema object and reads the server's back at the handshake, so neither is
         coupled to a schema moving — and making five schema-agnostic packages re-release
         because the sixth had to is a cost with nothing on the other side.

         What a moved fingerprint *does* force is a **minor** bump on the package that
         states it, because an installed client carrying the old number is refused at the
         handshake and under `0.x` the minor is the breaking slot.
         `scripts/check-versions.py` enforces exactly that, against
         `clients/dotnet/PUBLISHED.tsv`; README.md's flag-day section is the surrounding
         procedure. -->
    <Version>0.6.2</Version>
    <Company>Boxops</Company>
    <Authors>Boxops</Authors>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/boxops-uk/fjord</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/boxops-uk/fjord</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>

    <!-- A published package should be traceable back to the commit that built it and
         steppable into. `PublishRepositoryUrl` and `EmbedUntrackedSources` are what make
         Source Link resolve for a consumer; the symbols travel as a separate `.snupkg`,
         which is what nuget.org expects. -->
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
